<!-- ============================================================
     VeilStealer: PowerShell-Based Credential Stealer
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK Techniques:
       T1566.001  - Phishing: Spearphishing Attachment (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1059.001  - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell (TA0002 Execution)
       T1555.003  - Credentials from Password Stores: Credentials from Web Browsers (TA0006)
       T1005      - Data from Local System (TA0009 Collection)
       T1119      - Automated Collection (TA0009 Collection)
       T1140      - Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information (TA0005 Defense Evasion)
       T1041      - Exfiltration Over C2 Channel (TA0010 Exfiltration)
       T1071.001  - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (TA0011 C2)
     Rule IDs: 104184-104191
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team, ESKA-1076)
     Date: 2026-11-02
     Source: CYFIRMA Weekly Intelligence Report, July 24, 2026

     Threat Summary:

     VeilStealer is a PowerShell-based information stealer first documented
     in the wild on July 24, 2026. Key behavioral characteristics:

       - Delivered via phishing lure email (T1566.001): attacker sends a
         malicious attachment that executes PowerShell in-memory (T1059.001).
       - Browser credential harvesting (T1555.003): reads Chrome, Firefox,
         and Edge saved-password databases directly from the user profile.
         Target paths:
           Chrome:   AppData\Local\Google\Chrome\User Data\Default\Login Data
           Firefox:  AppData\Roaming\Mozilla\Firefox\Profiles\ (logins.json)
           Edge:     AppData\Local\Microsoft\Edge\User Data\Default\Login Data
       - Document enumeration (T1005, T1119): uses Get-ChildItem recursively
         with filename filters for common sensitive document patterns
         (invoice, password, contract, credentials, secret).
       - Low-footprint design: runs in-memory with minimal disk artifacts,
         no binary payload dropped between harvest and exfiltration.
       - Exfiltration via HTTP POST (T1041): uses Invoke-WebRequest or
         Invoke-RestMethod to POST harvested credentials and documents to
         attacker-controlled C2. Also observed using certutil for data
         encoding prior to transmission (T1140).

     No specific file hashes confirmed (early July 24, 2026 intelligence);
     detection relies on behavioral signatures.

     Detection Approach:

       104184 (Level 5)  - Anchor: powershell.exe command referencing any
                           browser credential store path. Used as base for
                           frequency rule 104191; fires silently.
       104185 (Level 13) - Chrome Login Data accessed by powershell.exe
                           directly in the command line (T1555.003).
       104186 (Level 13) - Firefox profile credential database (logins.json)
                           accessed by powershell.exe (T1555.003).
       104187 (Level 13) - Edge or Brave credential database accessed by
                           powershell.exe (T1555.003).
       104188 (Level 13) - VeilStealer document enumeration: Get-ChildItem
                           with sensitive filename filters run by powershell
                           (invoice, password, contract, credentials, secret)
                           (T1005, T1119).
       104189 (Level 14) - certutil spawned by powershell.exe: used by
                           VeilStealer to encode harvested data for
                           transmission or to download secondary payloads
                           (T1140, T1105).
       104190 (Level 13) - PowerShell HTTP POST exfiltration: Invoke-WebRequest,
                           Invoke-RestMethod, or .NET WebClient POST method
                           in powershell.exe command line (T1041, T1071.001).
       104191 (Level 15) - Multi-browser credential sweep: 3 or more matches
                           of rule 104184 within 60 seconds from the same
                           PowerShell process - active credential harvesting
                           across multiple browsers (T1555.003).

     Log Source Prerequisites:

     All rules target Sysmon EventID 1 (ProcessCreate) - Wazuh SID 61603.
     Required Sysmon configuration: ProcessCreate events with CommandLine
     logging enabled. Sysmon must be deployed with a configuration that
     captures full command lines for powershell.exe and certutil.exe.

     Rule 104191 requires Sysmon EventID 1 forwarded to Wazuh with
     the win.eventdata.image field populated (standard Sysmon deployment).

     Note on detection coverage: Rules 104185-104187 fire when the
     credential database path appears literally in the PowerShell command
     line (e.g., inline -Command argument). If VeilStealer obfuscates
     paths via base64 encoding, detection falls back to 104190 (exfiltration)
     and 104189 (certutil encoding step). Both coverage paths are needed
     for full kill-chain visibility.

     References:
       - CYFIRMA Weekly Intelligence Report, July 24, 2026
       - MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1555.003: Credentials from Web Browsers
       - MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1059.001: PowerShell
       - ESKA-650, ESKA-1076
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="threat_intel,credential_access,execution,windows,veilstealer,infostealer,powershell,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Anchor: PowerShell referencing any browser credential store path
       Rule ID: 104184
       Base rule used for frequency correlation in rule 104191.
       Fires at Level 5 (informational - no direct alert generated for
       most environments) when powershell.exe or pwsh.exe command line
       contains a browser credential database path. Individual higher-
       severity rules (104185-104187) also fire for analyst visibility.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104184" level="5">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data|\\Mozilla\\Firefox\\Profiles\\|\\logins\.json|\\Microsoft\\Edge\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data|\\BraveSoftware\\Brave-Browser\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data)</field>
    <description>VeilStealer anchor: $(win.eventdata.image) command references browser credential store path on $(win.eventdata.computer) - informational anchor for frequency correlation (T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>veilstealer_anchor,browser_creds_ps,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1555.003 - Chrome Login Data access by powershell.exe
       Rule ID: 104185
       powershell.exe command line contains the Chrome saved-password
       database path (Login Data SQLite file). VeilStealer reads this
       file to extract usernames, URLs, and encrypted passwords.
       Chrome must be closed or the stealer copies the locked file
       to a temp location first.
       Level 13 - high-confidence browser credential harvesting.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104185" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)\\Google\\Chrome\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data</field>
    <description>VeilStealer: $(win.eventdata.image) accessing Chrome credential database on $(win.eventdata.computer) - PowerShell reading Chrome Login Data for credential harvesting (T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,browser_creds_ps,veilstealer,chrome_creds,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1555.003 - Firefox credential database access by powershell.exe
       Rule ID: 104186
       powershell.exe command line references the Firefox Profiles
       directory or logins.json file where Firefox stores saved
       passwords. VeilStealer targets logins.json alongside the
       key4.db master password database.
       Level 13 - high-confidence browser credential harvesting.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104186" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\Mozilla\\Firefox\\Profiles\\|\\logins\.json)</field>
    <description>VeilStealer: $(win.eventdata.image) accessing Firefox credential database on $(win.eventdata.computer) - PowerShell reading Firefox logins.json for credential harvesting (T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,browser_creds_ps,veilstealer,firefox_creds,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1555.003 - Edge or Brave credential database access by powershell.exe
       Rule ID: 104187
       powershell.exe command line references the Microsoft Edge or
       Brave Browser credential database (Login Data file in the
       user profile AppData path). VeilStealer targets all Chromium-
       based browsers using the same SQLite credential storage format.
       Level 13 - high-confidence browser credential harvesting.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104187" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(\\Microsoft\\Edge\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data|\\BraveSoftware\\Brave-Browser\\User Data\\Default\\Login Data)</field>
    <description>VeilStealer: $(win.eventdata.image) accessing Edge or Brave credential database on $(win.eventdata.computer) - PowerShell reading Chromium-based browser Login Data for credential harvesting (T1555.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,browser_creds_ps,veilstealer,edge_creds,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1005 / T1119 - VeilStealer document enumeration pattern
       Rule ID: 104188
       powershell.exe invoked with Get-ChildItem (or gci/dir) and
       -Include or -Filter parameters targeting sensitive document
       filename patterns: invoice, password, contract, credentials,
       secret, backup, private, key. VeilStealer uses recursive
       document sweep to stage files for exfiltration alongside
       browser credentials. The -Recurse flag combined with sensitive
       filename patterns is the distinctive behavioral signature.
       Level 13 - automated sensitive document collection.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104188" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(Get-ChildItem|gci|dir).*-[Rr]ecurse.*(invoice|password|contract|credentials|credential|secret|backup|private\.key|id_rsa)</field>
    <description>VeilStealer: $(win.eventdata.image) running recursive document enumeration with sensitive filename filters on $(win.eventdata.computer) - automated collection of sensitive documents for exfiltration (T1005, T1119)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1005</id>
      <id>T1119</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>collection,document_enum,veilstealer,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1140 / T1105 - certutil spawned by powershell.exe
       Rule ID: 104189
       certutil.exe launched with powershell.exe as parent process.
       VeilStealer uses certutil to base64-encode harvested credential
       data before HTTP exfiltration (-encode flag) or to decode and
       execute secondary payloads (-decode, -urlcache -split -f).
       Legitimate use cases for this parent-child relationship are
       rare outside of enterprise software packaging; treat as high
       confidence in most environments.
       Level 14 - living-off-the-land encode or download behavior.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104189" level="14">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\certutil\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <description>VeilStealer: certutil.exe spawned by $(win.eventdata.parentImage) on $(win.eventdata.computer) - PowerShell-spawned certutil used for credential data encoding or secondary payload download (T1140, T1105)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1140</id>
      <id>T1105</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>defense_evasion,exfiltration,certutil_ps,veilstealer,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1041 / T1071.001 - PowerShell HTTP POST exfiltration
       Rule ID: 104190
       powershell.exe command line containing Invoke-WebRequest (IWR),
       Invoke-RestMethod (IRM), or .NET WebClient with HTTP POST method
       and a body or upload parameter. VeilStealer exfiltrates harvested
       browser credentials and documents to attacker-controlled C2 via
       HTTP POST. The combination of -Method POST with -Body or -InFile
       in a PowerShell command is the exfiltration behavioral signature.
       Also catches the -UploadData and UploadFile WebClient patterns.
       Level 13 - outbound HTTP data upload from PowerShell.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104190" level="13">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(powershell|pwsh)\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.commandLine" type="pcre2">(?i)(Invoke-WebRequest|Invoke-RestMethod|\bIWR\b|\bIRM\b|\.UploadData\b|\.UploadFile\b|\.UploadString\b).{0,200}(-Method\s+POST|POST.{0,100}-Body|-Body.{0,100}-Method\s+POST|-InFile)</field>
    <description>VeilStealer: $(win.eventdata.image) performing HTTP POST data upload on $(win.eventdata.computer) - PowerShell Invoke-WebRequest or WebClient POST used for credential exfiltration to C2 (T1041, T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1041</id>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>exfiltration,http_post_ps,veilstealer,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       T1555.003 - Multi-browser credential sweep: 3 or more hits in 60s
       Rule ID: 104191
       Three or more matches of the base anchor rule 104184 within
       60 seconds from the same PowerShell process. VeilStealer sweeps
       all installed browsers in rapid succession: Chrome, Firefox, Edge,
       and Brave are all targeted in a single execution. This frequency
       rule catches the multi-browser sweep pattern even when individual
       browser rules fire at lower severity. Highest-confidence
       VeilStealer detection - active credential sweep in progress.
       Level 15 - near-certain active multi-browser credential harvesting.
       =========================================================== -->

  <rule id="104191" level="15" frequency="3" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>104184</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>win.eventdata.image</same_field>
    <description>VeilStealer ACTIVE SWEEP: $(win.eventdata.image) accessed 3 or more browser credential stores within 60 seconds on $(win.eventdata.computer) - multi-browser credential harvest in progress, immediate incident response required (T1555.003)</description>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1555.003</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,browser_creds_ps,veilstealer,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
